-- Migration: Question generation — AUD batch 30 (30 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-23
-- Topic: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies
-- Difficulty: 9E / 15M / 6H
-- Bloom's: L1:8 / L2:9 / L3:8 / L4:5

BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Clearly trivial misstatement threshold
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Meridian Software Corp., the auditor identifies a $200 error in office supply accruals. Performance materiality is $50,000 and overall materiality is $100,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Accumulate this misstatement on the summary of uncorrected misstatements", "Disregard the misstatement as clearly trivial without further accumulation", "Report the misstatement to those charged with governance as a significant deficiency", "Reduce performance materiality to account for the small error"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.05, the auditor need not accumulate misstatements that are clearly trivial—amounts well below materiality that are inconsequential individually and in aggregate. A $200 error against $100,000 materiality is clearly trivial. Choice A is incorrect because clearly trivial misstatements do not need to be accumulated on the summary schedule.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Iron curtain vs rollover approach for prior-period misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Brightfield Energy has a $120,000 uncorrected overstatement of revenue originating three years ago. The cumulative balance sheet effect remains material, but the current-year income statement effect is zero. Overall materiality is $200,000. The engagement partner must evaluate this misstatement using:', '["Only the income statement (rollover) approach because no current-year effect exists", "Only the balance sheet (iron curtain) approach because the cumulative effect persists", "Both the rollover and iron curtain approaches to assess potential material misstatement", "Neither approach because the misstatement relates to a prior period"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A16, the auditor considers the effect of uncorrected misstatements using both the rollover approach (current-year income effect) and the iron curtain approach (cumulative balance sheet effect). SAB 108 similarly requires a dual approach. Choice A is incorrect because ignoring the cumulative balance sheet effect could allow a materially misstated balance sheet to persist uncorrected.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Definition of clearly trivial
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under auditing standards, what is a clearly trivial misstatement?', '["A misstatement that is inconsequential both individually and in aggregate", "Any misstatement below performance materiality", "A misstatement that management has agreed to correct", "An error identified during interim testing procedures"]'::jsonb, 0, 'AU-C 450.05 defines clearly trivial misstatements as those that are inconsequential both individually and in aggregate, whether judged by size, nature, or circumstances. Choice B is incorrect because misstatements below performance materiality may still be significant enough to accumulate—clearly trivial is a much lower threshold than performance materiality.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Summary of uncorrected misstatements schedule purpose
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Cascade Manufacturing, the auditor accumulates seven individually immaterial misstatements totaling $45,000 against overall materiality of $150,000. Under AU-C 450, the primary purpose of maintaining a summary of uncorrected misstatements is to:', '["Provide management with a list of journal entries to record before year-end", "Satisfy documentation requirements for the engagement quality review", "Calculate the projected misstatement for each account balance tested", "Enable the auditor to evaluate whether uncorrected misstatements are material in aggregate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.06, the auditor accumulates identified misstatements during the audit so that the aggregate effect can be evaluated near completion. The summary schedule enables assessment of whether individually immaterial items are collectively material. Choice C is incorrect because projected misstatement calculations are performed during sampling procedures, not through the summary of uncorrected misstatements.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Evaluating indicators of management bias
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While reviewing uncorrected misstatements at Pinnacle Industries, the senior auditor observes that all eight uncorrected errors increase net income—four relate to revenue recognition timing and four to understated accrued liabilities. Individual amounts range from $5,000 to $18,000 against materiality of $250,000, with an aggregate total of $72,000. The auditor should:', '["Accept the misstatements because the aggregate is well below materiality", "Evaluate whether the pattern indicates possible management bias and consider fraud risk implications", "Propose adjusting entries for the four largest misstatements to reduce the aggregate below 25% of materiality", "Issue a qualified opinion because the one-directional pattern constitutes a known misstatement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A20, the auditor should consider whether uncorrected misstatements indicate possible bias by management. A consistent direction of misstatements increasing income may indicate intentional bias, potentially triggering fraud risk considerations under AU-C 240. Choice A is incorrect because even when the aggregate is below materiality, a systematic pattern favoring income requires professional skepticism about management''s intent.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Group audit control deficiency communication
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the group audit of Atlas Consolidated, the component auditor identifies a material weakness in the subsidiary''s revenue recognition controls. The group engagement partner has not yet communicated this deficiency. Under AU-C 600 and AU-C 265, the group engagement partner should:', '["Evaluate the deficiency''s significance to the group financial statements and communicate it to group governance if appropriate", "Direct the component auditor to communicate the deficiency only to subsidiary management", "Exclude the deficiency from the group communication because it was identified at the component level", "Require the subsidiary to remediate the deficiency before issuing the group audit report"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 600.42 and AU-C 265.11, the group engagement partner must evaluate control deficiencies identified at the component level for their significance to the group audit. Material weaknesses at a significant component typically warrant communication to those charged with governance of the group. Choice C is incorrect because control deficiencies at the component level are not automatically excluded from group-level communication.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Tolerable misstatement and audit strategy implications
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'At Redwood Financial Services, the auditor set overall materiality at $500,000, performance materiality at $375,000, and tolerable misstatement for accounts receivable at $200,000. Sample testing reveals projected misstatements of $180,000 in receivables. No other misstatements have been identified. The auditor should conclude that:', '["The accounts receivable balance is fairly stated because projected misstatements are below performance materiality", "The audit is complete because total identified misstatements do not exceed overall materiality", "Further procedures are needed because projected misstatements approach the tolerable misstatement for the account", "A qualified opinion is required because projected misstatements exceed 50% of performance materiality"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 530.A24, when projected misstatements approach or exceed the tolerable misstatement for an account, the risk that the actual misstatement exceeds tolerable misstatement is unacceptably high. The auditor should consider expanding procedures or requesting management correction. Choice A is incorrect because the relevant threshold for individual account evaluation is the tolerable misstatement assigned to that account, not overall performance materiality.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Communication timing for significant deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the interim audit of Starpoint Electronics, the auditor identifies a significant deficiency in the cash disbursement authorization process. The year-end audit work is not yet complete. Under AU-C 265, the auditor''s written communication of this significant deficiency should be made:', '["Immediately upon identification during interim fieldwork", "Within 30 calendar days of identification", "Only after confirming the deficiency has not been remediated by year-end", "No later than 60 days following the release of the auditor''s report"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.13, the auditor should communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance no later than 60 days following the release of the auditor''s report. Earlier communication is encouraged but not required. Choice A is incorrect because while timely oral communication may be appropriate, the written communication deadline is tied to the report release date, not the discovery date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Nature vs magnitude in qualitative evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Lakewood Pharmaceuticals, the auditor discovers a $3,000 misstatement in a related-party transaction disclosure. Overall materiality is $500,000. Although the amount is well below materiality, the auditor elevates this finding. Under AU-C 450, this decision is most likely because:', '["The nature of the misstatement involving related parties affects the qualitative evaluation", "Any misstatement in disclosures automatically requires a qualified opinion", "Related-party misstatements must always be communicated to regulatory authorities", "The auditor must accumulate all misstatements regardless of their nature"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.11, the auditor evaluates whether uncorrected misstatements are material individually or in aggregate, considering both size and nature. Related-party transaction misstatements carry qualitative significance that may influence user decisions even when quantitatively small. Choice D is incorrect because clearly trivial misstatements need not be accumulated; this finding warrants attention due to its qualitative nature, not a blanket accumulation requirement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Adjusting entries vs reclassifying entries
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While completing the audit of Granite Construction, the auditor proposes two types of corrections: a $40,000 entry to increase the allowance for doubtful accounts and a $75,000 entry to reclassify a current liability as long-term. Under AU-C 450, the distinction between these entries is important because:', '["Reclassifying entries do not need to be communicated to those charged with governance", "Only adjusting entries affect the auditor''s evaluation of aggregate misstatements", "Reclassifying entries affect classification and presentation but not the overall financial position total", "Adjusting entries must always be recorded while reclassifying entries are optional"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A4, misstatements include adjustments to amounts (adjusting entries) and reclassifications between line items. Reclassifying entries affect classification and disclosure but not aggregate totals like net income or total assets, which is relevant when evaluating aggregate effects. Choice B is incorrect because both adjusting and reclassifying entries are accumulated as misstatements in the auditor''s evaluation.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Deficiency in design definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is a deficiency in the design of an internal control?', '["A control that exists but fails to operate as intended by personnel", "A control that is missing or not properly designed to prevent or detect misstatements on a timely basis", "A weakness that has already resulted in a material misstatement of financial statements", "A control that was effective in the prior year but has not been updated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.07, a deficiency in design exists when a necessary control is missing or when an existing control is not properly designed to prevent or detect misstatements timely. Choice A describes a deficiency in operation rather than design—the control exists but personnel fail to apply it effectively.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Deficiency in operation vs design applied
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'At Silverline Technologies, the accounts payable department has a documented three-way match policy requiring purchase order, receiving report, and invoice agreement before payment. However, the auditor finds that staff regularly overrides this control to expedite vendor payments. Under AU-C 265, this situation represents:', '["A deficiency in operation because the properly designed control is not being executed effectively", "A deficiency in design because the three-way match policy does not adequately prevent misstatements", "Neither a deficiency in design nor operation because the policy exists in documented form", "A material weakness because any override of a preventive control is inherently significant"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.07, a deficiency in operation exists when a properly designed control does not operate effectively or when the person performing the control lacks the authority or competence to do so. The three-way match is well designed; the failure is in execution. Choice B is incorrect because the policy design is sound—the deficiency arises from staff not following the established procedure.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Segregation of duties deficiency evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Bayside Retail Corp., the auditor discovers that the bookkeeper who records cash receipts also performs the monthly bank reconciliation. No independent review of the reconciliation occurs. The auditor should evaluate this situation as:', '["An acceptable practice for a small company that cannot afford additional staff", "A deficiency in operation because the bookkeeper is performing the reconciliation incorrectly", "An immaterial finding because cash reconciliations are routine procedures", "A control deficiency related to inadequate segregation of duties that requires severity assessment"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A7, inadequate segregation of duties is a common control deficiency. When the same person records transactions and reconciles the related account, the risk of undetected errors or fraud increases. The auditor must assess severity under AU-C 265.08. Choice A is incorrect because entity size does not exempt an auditor from identifying and communicating control deficiencies, though compensating controls may mitigate severity.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: hard / L3 — IT general control deficiency cascading effect
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Westridge Banking Group, the auditor identifies that the IT department lacks a formal change management process for the general ledger system. Three unauthorized code modifications were made during the year. Multiple automated application controls depend on the general ledger system integrity. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should:', '["Evaluate only the three unauthorized changes without considering dependent application controls", "Assess the IT general control deficiency''s cascading effect on all dependent automated application controls", "Limit the evaluation to the financial applications directly modified by the unauthorized changes", "Classify the deficiency as significant only if the unauthorized changes caused actual misstatements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A12 and AU-C 315, IT general control deficiencies can undermine the reliability of dependent automated application controls. The auditor must consider the pervasive effect of change management failures on all systems relying on those IT controls. Choice D is incorrect because severity assessment considers the potential for misstatement, not just whether an actual misstatement has occurred.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L2 — Written representation about uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Greenfield Organic Foods, the auditor provides management with a list of six uncorrected misstatements totaling $28,000. Materiality is $200,000. Management declines to correct these items. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should obtain:', '["A revised engagement letter reflecting management''s refusal to adjust", "An oral confirmation from the CFO that the misstatements are immaterial", "A written representation from management that uncorrected misstatements are immaterial both individually and in aggregate", "Board of directors approval to waive the uncorrected misstatements"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.14, the auditor must request a written representation from management that uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole. This representation is included in the management representation letter. Choice B is incorrect because an oral confirmation does not satisfy the requirement—AU-C 450 specifically requires the representation to be in writing.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Prior year uncorrected misstatements in current evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the current-year audit of Thornton Medical Supplies, the auditor notes that $35,000 in misstatements from the prior year were not corrected and continue to affect the current-year opening balances. Overall materiality for the current year is $250,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Ignore the prior-year misstatements because they were evaluated in the previous audit", "Consider the prior-year uncorrected misstatements when evaluating the current year''s aggregate misstatements", "Require management to restate the prior-year financial statements before continuing", "Issue an adverse opinion on the current year because the opening balances contain misstatements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.10, the auditor evaluates the effect of uncorrected misstatements from prior periods on the current-period financial statements. Prior-year misstatements that carry forward affect current-year balances and must be included in the aggregate assessment. Choice A is incorrect because uncorrected prior-year misstatements do not cease to be relevant simply because they were previously evaluated.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Combination of deficiencies forming material weakness
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pacific Coast Distributors, the auditor identifies three control deficiencies: (1) no independent review of journal entries over $10,000, (2) lack of access controls for the accounting system, and (3) no reconciliation of intercompany accounts. Individually, each is assessed as a significant deficiency. All three relate to financial reporting oversight. The auditor should conclude:', '["The combination may constitute a material weakness because the deficiencies together increase the likelihood and magnitude of potential misstatement", "Each deficiency should be reported separately as a significant deficiency without further aggregation", "Only the journal entry review deficiency is relevant because it has the highest individual likelihood of misstatement", "The deficiencies cannot be combined because they involve different controls over different processes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A14, the auditor must consider whether individually less severe deficiencies, when combined, represent a material weakness. Deficiencies affecting the same financial reporting objective or area can interact to increase both the likelihood and potential magnitude of misstatement. Choice D is incorrect because deficiencies need not involve identical processes to be combined—their collective impact on financial reporting reliability is what matters.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Who communicates control deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, who is responsible for communicating control deficiencies identified during a financial statement audit?', '["The entity''s internal audit department", "The audit committee chair", "The entity''s chief financial officer", "The external auditor performing the engagement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.11–.12, the auditor is required to communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance and to communicate other deficiencies in writing to management. Choice A is incorrect because while internal audit may identify deficiencies, AU-C 265 places the communication responsibility on the external auditor conducting the financial statement audit.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Remediation during audit period
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Harbor View Insurance, the auditor identified a material weakness in claims processing controls in October. By December 31, management redesigned the controls and tested them for two months with no exceptions. The auditor verified the remediation. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should:', '["Omit the material weakness from the written communication because it was fully remediated before year-end", "Report the deficiency as a significant deficiency because remediation reduced its severity", "Still communicate the material weakness as identified during the audit period, noting remediation actions taken", "Defer communication until the following year to confirm the remediation remains effective"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A18, deficiencies that have been remediated during the audit period are still communicated because those charged with governance should be aware of conditions that existed. The auditor may note the remediation in the communication. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 265 requires communication of identified material weaknesses regardless of subsequent remediation during the audit period.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Definition of projected misstatement
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is a projected misstatement in the context of audit sampling?', '["The auditor''s best estimate of misstatement in a population based on errors found in a sample", "A misstatement management expects to occur in the next fiscal year", "An error that the auditor anticipates finding during substantive testing", "The total of all confirmed misstatements identified during the audit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 530.A20, a projected misstatement is the auditor''s best estimate of misstatement in the entire population, derived by extrapolating errors found in a sample to the full population. Choice D is incorrect because that describes known or factual misstatements, not the statistical projection from sample results to the population.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Communication of other (lesser) deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, to whom must the auditor communicate control deficiencies that are not significant deficiencies or material weaknesses?', '["The Securities and Exchange Commission", "The entity''s external legal counsel", "Those charged with governance in writing", "Management of the entity in writing"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.12, the auditor is required to communicate in writing to management other deficiencies in internal control identified during the audit that have not been communicated by others and that are of sufficient importance to merit management''s attention. Choice C is incorrect because written communication to governance is required for significant deficiencies and material weaknesses, not for lesser deficiencies.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Known misstatement definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What does the term "known misstatement" refer to in auditing standards?', '["A misstatement that management has disclosed to the auditor voluntarily", "A misstatement that has been specifically identified and precisely measured", "Any misstatement that exceeds the overall materiality threshold", "A misstatement communicated in a predecessor auditor''s report"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A3, a known misstatement (also called a factual misstatement) is one about which there is no doubt—it has been specifically identified through audit procedures and its amount is precisely determinable. Choice A is incorrect because the term refers to the certainty and measurability of the misstatement, not to how it was initially discovered.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Disabled control affecting audit strategy
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Summit Aerospace, the auditor discovers that management disabled the automated three-way matching control in the procurement system for three months during a system migration. During this period, $12 million in purchases were processed without automated matching. The auditor had planned a controls reliance strategy for accounts payable. Under AU-C 265 and AU-C 330, the auditor should:', '["Maintain the controls reliance strategy because the automated control was only disabled temporarily", "Report the deficiency as a significant deficiency and continue with the planned approach", "Reassess the audit strategy to increase substantive testing for the affected period and evaluate the deficiency as a potential material weakness", "Withdraw from the engagement because the internal control environment has been compromised"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 330.13, when a control on which the auditor planned to rely is not operating effectively, the auditor must revise the planned approach, typically by increasing substantive procedures. Under AU-C 265.08, disabling a key automated control for $12 million in transactions warrants material weakness evaluation. Choice A is incorrect because a three-month gap in a key control invalidates the reliance strategy for that period regardless of whether the control was later restored.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Purpose of performance materiality
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is the purpose of establishing performance materiality in the audit?', '["To determine the maximum amount of audit fees the client will pay", "To set the threshold above which all misstatements require a qualified opinion", "To reduce the risk that aggregate uncorrected misstatements exceed overall materiality", "To define the minimum sample size for all substantive procedures"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 320.09, performance materiality is set below overall materiality to reduce to an appropriately low level the probability that the aggregate of uncorrected and undetected misstatements exceeds materiality. It provides a buffer for undetected misstatements. Choice B is incorrect because the opinion decision is based on overall materiality, and a qualified opinion is not automatically triggered by any single threshold.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Fraud vs error distinction
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under auditing standards, what distinguishes a misstatement arising from fraud versus one arising from error?', '["Whether the underlying act causing the misstatement was intentional or unintentional", "Whether the misstatement exceeds or falls below materiality", "Whether the misstatement was discovered by the auditor or by management", "Whether the misstatement affects the income statement or the balance sheet"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 240.02 and AU-C 450.A2, the primary distinction is intent. A misstatement from fraud involves intentional acts such as fraudulent financial reporting or misappropriation of assets, whereas error involves unintentional misstatements. Choice B is incorrect because both fraud and error can produce misstatements of any amount—intent, not magnitude, is the distinguishing factor.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Financial statement-level control deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Crossroads Financial Group, the auditor determines that the CFO prepares the financial statements without any independent review or approval before issuance. The company has adequate transaction-level controls. The auditor should evaluate this finding as:', '["An acceptable practice because transaction-level controls adequately prevent misstatements", "A control deficiency at the financial statement level that warrants assessment for severity as a potential material weakness", "A deficiency in design that is automatically classified as a material weakness", "An immaterial finding because the CFO is a qualified financial professional"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A10, the absence of an independent financial statement review is a financial statement-level control deficiency that can allow misstatements in presentation and disclosure to go undetected. The auditor must assess severity under AU-C 265.08. Choice A is incorrect because transaction-level controls address individual account assertions but may not catch errors in overall presentation, disclosure, or aggregation of financial statements.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Magnitude component of deficiency severity
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Crestline Automotive, the auditor identifies a control deficiency in the inventory count process. The inventory account balance is $8 million, and the deficiency could result in misstatements up to $150,000. Overall materiality is $200,000. Under AU-C 265, when evaluating the magnitude component of this deficiency, the auditor should consider:', '["Only the actual misstatement amount discovered during testing", "The total inventory account balance of $8 million", "The deficiency''s impact relative to the auditor''s fee for the engagement", "The maximum potential misstatement that could result from the deficiency, not just the amount actually detected"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A8, the magnitude component considers the maximum amount of misstatement that could result from the control deficiency, not merely the misstatement amount actually identified. A $150,000 potential misstatement against $200,000 materiality suggests significant severity. Choice A is incorrect because limiting evaluation to actual detected misstatements understates the deficiency''s potential impact.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Compensating controls reducing deficiency severity
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Valley Creek Manufacturing, the auditor identifies that no formal authorization process exists for purchase orders under $5,000. However, the controller reviews all monthly expense reports line by line and investigates unusual items. When evaluating the severity of the authorization deficiency, the auditor should:', '["Ignore the controller''s review because it is a detective control rather than a preventive control", "Classify the deficiency as a material weakness because the primary authorization control is absent", "Consider whether the controller''s review is effective enough to reduce the likelihood and magnitude of potential misstatement", "Report the deficiency to those charged with governance without considering compensating controls"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A9, the auditor considers whether other controls (compensating controls) achieve the same objective when evaluating severity. If the controller''s line-by-line review effectively prevents or detects unauthorized purchases, it may reduce the deficiency''s severity. Choice B is incorrect because classifying a deficiency as material weakness requires evaluating all relevant controls, including compensating controls, not solely the absence of the primary control.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Documentation requirements for identified misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Oakmont Healthcare, the auditor identifies 14 misstatements across various accounts. Management corrects 10 and declines to correct 4 totaling $22,000. Materiality is $300,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor''s documentation should include:', '["Only the four uncorrected misstatements because corrected items are no longer relevant", "All misstatements identified during the audit, whether corrected or uncorrected, and the conclusion on materiality", "Only misstatements exceeding 10% of materiality to avoid excessive documentation", "A summary of uncorrected misstatements with no requirement to document corrected items"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.15, the auditor must document all accumulated misstatements identified during the audit (including those subsequently corrected), the conclusion as to whether uncorrected misstatements are material, and the basis for that conclusion. Choice A is incorrect because corrected misstatements remain relevant for understanding the nature and cause of errors, evaluating management bias, and assessing overall audit quality.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Uncorrected misstatements approaching materiality and report impact
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Northgate Retail, the auditor determines that management will not correct a $180,000 understatement of cost of goods sold. Overall materiality is $200,000 and the misstatement is pervasive across inventory accounts. Under AU-C 450 and AU-C 705, the auditor should consider:', '["Issuing an unmodified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph about the misstatement", "Withdrawing from the engagement because management refuses to cooperate", "Issuing a qualified opinion only if the misstatement exceeds materiality", "Whether the misstatement''s proximity to materiality and pervasiveness warrant a modification to the auditor''s report"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.12 and AU-C 705.08, when uncorrected misstatements approach materiality and are pervasive, the auditor must consider whether the financial statements as a whole are materially misstated. A $180,000 misstatement against $200,000 materiality with pervasive effects may warrant a qualified or adverse opinion. Choice C is incorrect because materiality is not a bright-line test—the auditor uses judgment considering both quantitative proximity and qualitative factors like pervasiveness.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
